{
  "requirement": "Usage MUST record mandatory input counts and sizes by evidence kind, in UTF-8\nbytes and lines, and the same information for additional inputs requested through\n`habeas` or `ripgrep`. A requirements input MUST also record its number of matched declarations.\nUsage MUST distinguish unique inputs from repeated reads, successful from\nfailed reads, and prepared inputs from those actually sent to the model.\nFile-discovery calls MUST count as tool calls without recording directory\nlistings as evidence or source inputs.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the requirement is satisfied.** I found no unacknowledged gaps, so I did not use FIXME. The one reservation is that tests check the exclusion of directory listings from usage indirectly, and there are small marking omissions. Neither changes the judgment.\n\n**Implementation**\n\n- **Mandatory inputs, by kind, in bytes and lines.**\n  - `configured_record` calls `usage.prepared(\"mandatory\", sources.mandatory_inputs())` and saves the log before the provider connects.\n  - `UsageLog::prepared` (`src/authoring/usage.rs`) writes one `InputUsage` per input with `origin`, `kind`, `bytes`, `lines`, `components`, `unique`, `submitted_in_request` and `sent_in_request`.\n  - `kind` is `outline` (input has `item`), `requirements`, or `definition`.\n  - `bytes` is the UTF-8 length of the text lines plus separating newlines. `lines` is the number of text lines.\n  - The mandatory input shapes built in `AssessmentSources::prepare` (requirements, outline items, definitions) all map onto these kinds.\n- **Additional inputs through `habeas` or `ripgrep`.** In `assess`, each successful dispatch calls `usage.prepared(call.function.name, &delivery.inputs)`. The delivered inputs include the terminology definitions that `read` and `search` return, so the same fields are recorded with `origin` set to the tool name.\n- **Matched declarations.** `components` is the length of `matches`, and is 1 when `matches` is absent.\n- **Unique versus repeated reads.** `unique` is set from a `seen` set keyed on the serialized input, so repeats across mandatory and tool origins are flagged.\n- **Successful versus failed reads.** `usage.tool(name, failed)` increments `tool_calls` and, on failure, `tool_failures`. `skipped_tool` covers calls ignored because they arrived alongside a submission. Unknown tool names collapse to `unknown`, so model text does not leak into the log.\n- **Prepared versus actually sent.** `begin_request` stamps `submitted_in_request` on every unstamped input. `end_request` stamps `sent_in_request` only when the provider response carried usage, that is, the request completed. Inputs from a failed attempt therefore stay unsent until a later attempt completes.\n- **File discovery.** `list_files`, like `outline` and `references`, returns `inputs: Vec::new()`. `assess` still calls `usage.tool(\"list_files\", …)`, so the call counts as a tool call. `usage.prepared` is called with an empty slice, so no input is recorded. Those tools carry their own `{impl}` marks for the file-discovery requirement, which is assessed on its own.\n- **Marking.** `InputUsage`, `prepared`, `begin_request`, `end_request`, `tool`, `skipped_tool`, `configured_record` and `assess` all carry `{impl}` for `usage_inputs`. Two items lack that mark: the `inputs`, `tool_calls` and `tool_failures` fields of the `UsageRecord` struct, and the `tool_name` helper. Both only support these functions, so I treat this as a trivial omission.\n- **Unspecified behavior.** I found none beyond the requirement and the sibling usage requirements (`usage_storage`, `usage_requests`).\n\n**Tests**\n\n- `input_metadata_counts_components_and_reads_without_persisting_source` checks:\n  - kind, UTF-8 byte count (the string includes `λ`) and line count;\n  - `components == 2` for a requirements input with two matches;\n  - `unique` true then false for a repeated `habeas` read, with the origin recorded;\n  - `submitted_in_request` and `sent_in_request` moving from null to set;\n  - that no source text or paths reach the log.\n- `failed_submission_can_be_confirmed_on_a_later_attempt` checks that a failed request leaves `sent_in_request` null and a later completed request sets it.\n- `throttled_requests_retry_unchanged_and_record_every_attempt` checks, over 8 failures and a ninth completed request, that inputs have `submitted_in_request` 0 and `sent_in_request` 8.\n- `tool_outcomes_are_distinct_and_unknown_names_do_not_leak_model_text` checks calls, failures and skips counted separately.\n- The CLI test `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free` runs `list_files`, `ripgrep`, a repeated `habeas` read and a failing `habeas` read end to end. It asserts a total of 6 tool calls, 3 `habeas` calls with 1 failure, a non-unique `habeas` input with `sent_in_request == 1`, and a mandatory `definition` input with non-zero bytes. It asserts that no listing appears among the evidence inputs, and that no source or credential content is in the usage log.\n- Tool-level tests (`tools.rs`) assert that `list_files` returns no inputs.\n- The tests do not directly assert a `list_files` count in the usage log, or that the log has no listing entry. The total of 6 tool calls and the empty-inputs assertions imply both, which I judge adequate.",
  "inputs": [
